{"tag":"KV0P","body":"<div class=\"pq-q ue-math\"><p>In a society <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"N,\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8778em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.109em;\">N</span></span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span></span></span></span></span> each individual chooses a single object from a set <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"X,\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8778em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span></span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span></span></span></span></span> interpreted as the social action he recommends. Consider functions that aggregate the individuals’ recommendations (not preferences, just recommendations) into a social decision, and the axioms:</p><p>Par: if all individuals recommend <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"x*,\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8831em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.6887em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mbin mtight\">∗</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span></span></span></span></span> society chooses <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"x*;\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8831em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.6887em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mbin mtight\">∗</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mpunct\">;</span></span></span></span></span></p><p>I: if the same individuals recommend an alternative <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"x\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span></span></span></span></span></span> in two profiles of recommendations, then <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"x\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span></span></span></span></span></span> is chosen in one profile if and only if it is chosen in the other.</p></div><ol class=\"pq-parts\" role=\"list\"><li class=\"pq-part\"><span class=\"pq-l\">(a)</span><div class=\"pq-pq\"><div class=\"ue-math\"><p>Show that if <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"X\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span></span></span></span></span></span> has at least three elements, the only aggregation method that satisfies Par and I is a dictatorship. (b) Show that each of the three conditions Par, I and <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"|X|≥3\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">∣</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0785em;\">X</span><span class=\"mord\">∣</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≥</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span></span></span></span></span></span> is needed for this conclusion.</p></div><div class=\"pq-ans\" data-answer><p class=\"pq-ans-k\">Answer</p><div class=\"ue-math\"><p>Shown: with at least three actions, the aggregation methods satisfying Par and I are exactly the dictatorships, “dictatorship” meaning that one individual’s recommendation is always the social choice.</p></div></div></div></li><li class=\"pq-part\"><span class=\"pq-l\">(b)</span><div class=\"pq-pq\"><div class=\"pq-ans\" data-answer><p class=\"pq-ans-k\">Answer</p><div class=\"ue-math\"><p>Par is needed: some method satisfies I without being a dictatorship. For instance, always choosing the same action, whatever is recommended, satisfies I.</p></div></div></div></li></ol>"}